  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Skies gradually clearing this morning as mid-\nlevel ridge centered over the southern plains builds eastward,\nshunting deeper moisture offshore. At the surface, weak high\npressure is now in control with weekend front out of the\npicture, keeping winds light. With little change in moisture\nfrom yesterday, expecting another round of fog and low stratus\nespecially west of Highway 17. Some periods of locally dense fog\nare possible early this AM.\n\nWith ridge shifting overhead increasing mid-level subsidence, \nthe risk of more widespread convection will largely be quashed. \nSea and sound breeze activity today may spark a short lived \nshower or storm in the afternoon, but otherwise area will remain\ndry as highs climb back into the 90s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Ridging aloft builds further early this week \nand peaks over the area Wednesday and Thursday with H85 temps \naround 21-22C leading to temps well in the 90s most areas away \nfrom the immediate coast. Heat headlines may be needed \nWednesday, Thursday, and possibly lingering into Friday with \nforecast heat indices climbing above 105 each day, especially \ninland areas and along the coast south of US70. Some locations \nmay approach record high temperature values mid week and have \nincluded the current record values in the CLIMATE section below.\n\nSubsidence aloft will limit thunderstorm activity for much of\nthe week with only isolated afternoon convection along the sea\nbreeze at best, but most areas are expected to remain dry\nthrough Thursday. The ridge begins to retreat westward late in \nthe week which NW flow developing aloft bringing the potential \nfor MCV's diving from the Mid-West across the area, possibly as \nearly as Thursday night but more likely late Friday into the\nweekend. Still plenty of details to sort out, but AI NWP\nguidance suggests the pattern during this time will be at least\nmarginally favorable for strong to severe storms.",
